Each check day is one job at a time, as in the Course-level checks bullet: the written part first (every student
seated, the teacher reading or dancing the unseen piece twice, 10 minutes), then six groups of five dance for about
three minutes each while the rest sit as the audience; set-up, warm-up, cool-down and room-back fit the rest of the
40 minutes. Every student's evidence is collected once in a window; no item is scored twice. Days above are band
course days (year 4 starts on day 65, year 5 on day 129); the unit record days named are the unit's last two days
before its respond day, as the scope and sequence sets them.

## Cross-subject

The three dance technique rows of grades 3 to 5 carry a lean on physical education, da-g3-respond on ELA's SL.3.1
and da-g5-respond on ELA's W.5.1 (data/progressions/arts.csv); no other row carries a cross_subject lean. The
connecting rows of grades 4 and 5 dance a topic another course teaches ("the topic's own course, not this one,
teaches the topic"), so science is named too. All are core courses except physical education and health, after-core
courses named by their own frames (lesson 32: this course assumes no motor cue or health lesson taught there).

### Physical education, grades 3 to 5 (same year, name each other)

The physical education grades 3 to 5 frame is written; its units and weeks are cited from it and from
data/plans/calendar.csv.

| This course's row and unit | Physical education's row and unit | What each teaches | If later |
|---|---|---|---|
| da-g3-technique, da-g3-elements (unit 1, grade 3 weeks 1 to 8) | g3-dance-steps (unit 8, grade 3 weeks 30 to 32) | Physical education: dance steps in four-count phrases as motor skill; dance: alignment, partner safety, recall, space, time and energy | Dance teaches first; physical education unit 8 may retrieve this unit's space and energy words. If a school runs it earlier, the dance unit retrieves its steps as material and still teaches only the art |
| da-g4-technique, da-g4-elements (unit 5, grade 4 weeks 1 to 8); da-g4-form (unit 7, grade 4 weeks 18 to 25) | g4-dance-sequence, g4-dance-ideas (unit 15, grade 4 weeks 27 to 30) | Physical education: a linked dance sequence and movement ideas as motor skill; dance: balance, cues, rhythm, energy words and a dance study with altered tools | Dance teaches first; physical education unit 15 opens with a retrieval task on the elements. The dance study never asks for physical education's beginning, middle and end cues as motor skill |
| da-g5-technique, da-g5-elements (unit 9, grade 5 weeks 1 to 9); da-g5-form (unit 11, grade 5 weeks 19 to 25) | g5-gym-dance-sequence (unit 22, grade 5 weeks 22 to 25) | Physical education: an eight-part routine as motor skill; dance: an ensemble run of phrases, body percussion and a study whose tools change together | Dance unit 9 teaches first; unit 11 runs in the same weeks as physical education unit 22 and uses only the class's own moves, so neither needs the other |

### Science, grades 4 and 5 (same year: the topic danced)

| Used here | Contact | If science has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|
| Unit 6 (grade 4 weeks 9 to 16): a school topic from two or three sources, danced (DA:Cn10.1.4b) | Science grade 4 unit 1 (days 1 to 15) and science grade 4 unit 2 (days 16 to 38) are taught by then; science grade 4 unit 3 (days 42 to 56) teaches waves in these weeks | The group picks unit 1's or unit 2's topic; waves only once science has taught them. The source cards carry only facts from the science course's own readings, and the dance unit teaches no science |
| Unit 10 (grade 5 weeks 9 to 17): another subject's topic, danced and compared (DA:Cn10.1.5b) | Science grade 5 unit 1 (days 1 to 17) teaches particles of matter in solids, liquids and gases | Taught before this unit. If a school orders science differently, the group picks any science topic its class has finished, and the comparison line still compares the two ways of learning |

### English language arts, grades 3 to 5 (same year)

| Used here | Contact | If ELA has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|
| Unit 3 (grade 3 weeks 18 to 25): class talk about two traditions (da-g3-respond, ela:SL.3.1) | English language arts grade 3 unit 1 (days 1 to 39) teaches collaborative discussion (SL.3.1) | Taught before this unit. Practiced, not owned: the critique card shows the talk rules (one at a time, build on what was said, ask a question) |
| Unit 7 (grade 4): class talk about styles | SL.4.1 in English language arts grade 4 | Practiced, not owned; the critique card as above |
| Unit 11 (grade 5 weeks 19 to 25): a written opinion with reasons on what makes a dance meaningful (da-g5-respond, ela:W.5.1) | English language arts grade 5 unit 3 (days 84 to 123) teaches opinion writing (W.5.1) in the same weeks | Practiced, not owned: the unit's opinion frame (my opinion, two reasons, a move from the dance for each) is taught on the day it is used, and the item marks the reasons, never the writing conventions; grade 4's W.4.1 opinion writing is the year before's |

### Health and physical education's food unit (contact only)

DA:Pr5.1.4b and DA:Pr5.1.5b join technique, injury and food choices. Health grades 3 to 5 unit 9 (grade 4 weeks 5
to 9) teaches preventing illness and injury, and physical education grades 3 to 5 unit 20 (grade 5 weeks 15 to 17)
teaches food and drink for moving. Neither is assumed: units 5 and 9 study the dance link themselves through an
invented dancer, and teach no health lesson.

### Mathematics, grades 3 to 5 (same year)

| Used here | Contact | If math has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|
| Units 1, 5 and 9: counting beats and set counts of 8 and 16; steady and uneven rhythms | Counting is long taught | Counts are said and clapped; no number is written, added or divided |
| Units 4, 8 and 12: floor maps, relationship sketches and stage plans | Grade 5 math's coordinate plane (5.G.A.1) | Maps use arrows and symbols, and plans are paced out in steps; no grid, scale, measuring or coordinates |

No unit asks for fractions, note values, time signatures, measuring or scale (lesson 13).

### Social studies, grades 3 to 5

Units 2, 6 and 10 teach a dance's setting (Martinique, the South African mines, Harlem and the rural South in the
1930s and 1940s) on their own work cards (bar criterion 7). No lean either way, and no student is asked to explain
their own family's culture.

