23: 
24: ## 1. The standard
25: 
26: - National Core Arts Standards, 2014 (National Coalition for Core Arts Standards), the performance standards.
27: - Loaded in data/standards/ncas-performance-standards.txt as ids only, no wording: 1,264 ids across the five
28:   disciplines (visual arts 195, music 264, theater 298, dance 273, media arts 234), from the 2021-11 "at a glance"
29:   handouts, fetched 2026-10-27, and the 2021-11 music composition and theory strand handout, downloaded and read
30:   2026-11-21 (Cory, Desk q-fix-downloads-1). Format `DIS:<process><anchor>.<standard>.<grade><letter>`, for example
31:   `DA:Cr1.1.2a`. High school levels are `.Ia`, `.IIa`, `.IIIa` (media arts cells with one statement carry no
32:   letter, as `MA:Re9.1.I`).
33: - Eleven anchor standards in four artistic processes: creating (Cr1 to Cr3), performing, presenting or producing
34:   (Pr4 to Pr6), responding (Re7 to Re9), connecting (Cn10, Cn11). The bar asks for all four processes in every
35:   course (criterion 1).
36: - scripts/standards.py checks NCAS codes by a closed pattern on discipline, process and anchor, and accepts the
37:   strand letter and the grade or level (read 2026-11-19), and checks every NCAS id against the id file (data/plans/
38:   later.csv L83, done 2026-11-21). Each scope and sequence still states its id count against the table in section
39:   3.2, and a reader checks that each id's meaning is taught, which the script cannot see.
40: - Music is loaded for pre-kindergarten to grade 8 general music and for the high school composition and theory
41:   strand (63 ids, loaded 2026-11-21; Cory, Desk q-fix-arts-1 and q-fix-downloads-1), as section 3.3 says.
42: 
43: ## 2. Reuse terms (read at source, 2026-11-16)
44: 
45: Read on the coalition's own copyright guidelines (nationalartsstandards.org, "NCAS Copyright Guidelines", the
46: 2025 posting of a document dated 2016). Research notes, not legal advice.
47: 
48: - The notice: the standards are copyright 2014 by the coalition, rights administered by Young Audiences, Inc., all
49:   rights reserved.
50: - Free and unlimited use covers only the eleven anchor standards and the blank framework matrix.
51: - Everything else (the performance standards, enduring understandings, essential questions, model cornerstone
52:   assessments, glossary) needs permission to reproduce, with set exceptions: teachers may copy the standards as
53:   shown on the site for their own teaching, and anyone may quote up to 250 words with the citation.
54: - Paraphrase is allowed in general, with the citation.
55: - What Curio Class does: the file and every plan carry ids and our own words only. No performance standard,
56:   enduring understanding or essential question is copied into a page, a day or a tool. Our own essential
57:   questions are written fresh. A page that names the standards gives the citation line in the notice's form.
58: - The model cornerstone assessments are not adapted. Our end-of-unit assessments are our own.
59: - A plan names a performance standard by id and states its action in one line of our own words. It never quotes
60:   the performance standards. The eleven anchors may be quoted in their real wording. Decided (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-arts-1):
61:   a one-line words file for the taught ids (id, tab, at most 14 words of what the student does, never a
62:   re-sentence of the standards' phrasing), written by one agent and read by a second, in the public repository; the
63:   63 strand ids joined it 2026-11-21, so it holds all 1,176 taught ids. The file now exists (data/standards/ncas-performance-standards-words.txt,
64:   written 2026-11-20): a plan cites the id and uses the file's line for the action.
65: 
66: ## 3. The band-year rule
67: 
68: Our arts courses are bands: one course per discipline per band (visual arts, grades K to 2, and so on). The
69: standards are written grade by grade to grade 8, then in three high school levels. The rule below places them.
70: 
71: ### 3.1 Kindergarten to grade 8: the grade's own standards in the grade's own year
72: 
73: | Band | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 |
74: |---|---|---|---|
75: | K to 2 | Kindergarten ids (`.Ka` and so on) | Grade 1 ids | Grade 2 ids |
76: | 3 to 5 | Grade 3 ids | Grade 4 ids | Grade 5 ids |
77: | 6 to 8 | Grade 6 ids | Grade 7 ids | Grade 8 ids |
78: 
79: - Each year's scope and sequence lists every id of its grade in the discipline, unit by unit. Each id is taught
80:   and assessed in that year. The unit of count is the lettered id, one statement (`DA:Cr1.1.2a`); the table in
81:   3.2 gives each year's count. A year may revisit the year before's ids as practice that returns, but it claims
82:   only its own grade.
83: - Music's connecting ids (`MU:Cn10.1`, `MU:Cn11.1`) are one statement repeated in every grade, so their words lines
84:   match from K to 8; each grade meets them with its own music and words, and the progression row says what changes.
85: - Pre-kindergarten ids are not taught. A kindergarten unit may use one as a readiness reference.
86: - Mixed-grade classes are common in the arts. A school that teaches a band as one mixed class runs the three
87:   years in turn. Each year still claims only its grade's ids, so a student's record shows which grade's standards
88:   they met. The scope and sequence says this once, on its first page.
89: 
90: ### 3.2 Grades 9 to 12: levels, not grades (Cory 2026-11-16, Desk card q-later-arts-health-pe)
91: 
92: The high school standards come in three levels, defined by what a student has done, not by grade (NAfME's
93: appendix to the music standards, read 2026-11-16, our words): Proficient is what most students reach in one high
94: school arts course of about 120 hours beyond good pre-kindergarten to grade 8 learning; Accomplished takes a
95: further sequence of courses; Advanced goes well past Accomplished, at honors or first-year college depth. The
96: evidence for the structure below is docs/reviews/arts-hs-music-and-levels-2026-11-16.md: no peer-reviewed study
97: compares sequenced and survey high school arts courses, so the structure follows the national descriptors and the
98: state frameworks that turn them into yearlong courses.
99: 
100: | Year of the 9 to 12 band | Level taught | Ids |
101: |---|---|---|
102: | Level 1 | Proficient | `.Ia` |
103: | Level 2 | Accomplished | `.IIa` |
104: | Level 3 | Advanced, at honors depth | `.IIIa` |
105: | Level 4 (optional) | Advanced, deepened: a portfolio or production year the student leads | `.IIIa`, assessed again on new, self-directed work |
106: 
107: - The 9 to 12 band is one course per discipline, as every other band is; its years are levels. A level is a
108:   full 160-day year, which is about the 120 hours the descriptor names.
109: - Entry: a student new to a discipline starts at Level 1, whatever their grade. The scope and sequence names the
110:   level, never a grade. Level 1's first unit assumes no earlier arts learning.
111: - Each level requires the one before, or a placement showing: the student shows work scored against the previous
112:   level's end-of-course assessment, and starts at the next level if it meets it.
113: - Advanced is claimed by assessed work, not by finishing Level 3. A student who ends Level 3 at "partly" has
114:   Level 4 to reach it.
115: - One level is a complete course and is enough for Proficient. The bar does not ask a school to offer all four.
116: - In data/progressions/arts.csv only, the `grade` column writes Level 1 as `9`, Level 2 as `10`, Level 3 as `11` and
117:   Level 4 as `12` (`~` only, on `.IIIa` ids), the key scripts/progression.py reads. It is a data key, never a
118:   student's grade: every frame, scope and sequence and page names the level. A row lists only the ids it owns or
119:   contributes to; earlier ids that return as practice are not listed.
120: - Short path (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-arts-1): each level's scope and sequence marks an 80-day path (a semester or a rotation) that keeps every
121:   unit and every id by merging the marked days. Level 1 opens with a bridge unit (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk
122:   q-fix-arts-1): it teaches the grade 8 techniques and words Level 1 needs, as readiness, and claims no grade 8 id;
123:   it is at most 12 days in the full year and 6 days on the 80-day short path, and its days count toward the bar's 10
124:   percent limit on days outside the course's standards (6 of 80 is 7.5 percent). Levels 2 and 3 open with a review
125:   unit of at most 6 days that doubles as placement; review days count as inside the standards. The frame lists them
126:   as bridge or review days. The bridge's at most 12 days in the full 160-day year is settled (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk
127:   q-fix-downloads-1): the same 7.5 percent share as its 6 days on the short path. The 6 days for review are ours,
128:   untested. The short
129:   path adds build work to the 15 high
130:   school levels (three in each of the five 9 to 12 courses), so many schools that cannot give an arts course every
131:   day can still use the course.
132: 
133: ### Ids owned by each course-year (counted from the id file, 2026-11-19, music 9 to 12 on 2026-11-21; pre-kindergarten excluded)
134: 
135: | Discipline | K to 2 (K, 1, 2) | 3 to 5 | 6 to 8 | 9 to 12, per level (`.Ia`, `.IIa`, `.IIIa`; Level 4 repeats `.IIIa`) |
136: |---|---|---|---|---|
137: | Visual arts (VA) | 15 + 15 + 15 = 45 | 45 | 45 | 15 |
138: | Music (MU) | 18 + 20 + 20 = 58 | 63 | 63 | 21 each, strand `.C`, 63 in all (3.3) |
139: | Theater (TH) | 17 + 24 + 24 = 65 | 72 | 72 | 24 |
140: | Dance (DA) | 21 + 21 + 21 = 63 | 63 | 63 | 21 |
141: | Media arts (MA) | 18 + 18 + 18 = 54 | 54 | 54 | 18 |
142: 
143: An id is one lettered statement (`DA:Cr1.1.2a`). A year's scope and sequence claims every id of its grade in its
144: discipline, counted in this table. The file holds 1,264 ids: 1,176 taught (K to 8 and the high school levels) and 88
145: pre-kindergarten, not taught. Totals of taught ids: visual arts 180, music 247, theater 281, dance 252, media arts
146: 216. The header's "per grade" figures are unlettered standards and are not the count. Music and theater
147: kindergarten are uneven (music K 18, grades 1 and 2 20, grades 3 to 8 21; theater K 17, grade 1 on 24), so no
148: year is counted by an even spread. High school music is one of the 20 courses, not an extra one; its 63 ids are
149: in the file since 2026-11-21.
150: 
151: ### 3.3 Music in high school: composition and theory (Cory 2026-11-16, Desk card q-later-arts-health-pe)
152: 
153: Music 9 to 12 is a full composition and theory course on the national composition and theory strand, not a stub
154: (settled 2026-11-16, Cory, Desk card q-later-followups-1).
155: The reasons and sources are in docs/reviews/arts-hs-music-and-levels-2026-11-16.md.
156: 
157: - The strand starts at Proficient (no Novice or Intermediate levels), so Level 1 follows grade 8 general music
158:   directly. It runs to Advanced in the same three levels as 3.2.
159: - It needs no instrument: work is shared by notation, performance or technology, and performances may be live
160:   or recorded. Days use the voice (singing, sight-singing), body and found percussion, a free keyboard on any
161:   device or a Curio Class tool, and the student's own instrument when they have one.
162: - Ensemble, harmonizing instrument and music technology ids are not claimed. Those strands assume instruments,
163:   a group or production tools and a teacher who hears technique in the room.
164: - An ensemble companion page sits inside the course (no days, no ids, not a counted course): how to find a
165:   school or community ensemble, a practice log and self-check, how an assignment can be written for the
166:   student's own instrument or section, and a plain note that ensemble standards are met and assessed by that
167:   ensemble.
168: - Ids: 21 per level, 63 in all, format `MU:Cr1.1.C.Ia` (the strand letter `C` before the level); the connecting
169:   ids keep the handout's anchor `.0` (`MU:Cn10.0.C.Ia`, `MU:Cn11.0.C.Ia`). scripts/standards.py accepts the strand
170:   letter (read 2026-11-19). Loaded 2026-11-21 (Cory, Desk q-fix-arts-1 and q-fix-downloads-1), ids only, from the
171:   coalition's strand handout downloaded and read once; each has its line in the words file (same form and limits),
172:   and data/progressions/arts.csv has the music Level 1 to 4 rows (`mu-g9-invent` to `mu-g12-connect`) in the form
173:   of the other disciplines' rows (3.2). The counts in 3.2 include them.
174: 
175: ### 3.4 Pacing (settled 2026-11-16)
176: 
177: The bar fails a plan that needs a daily arts period (criterion 9). A K to 8 arts year is 64 course days (two
178: sessions a week over 32 weeks, docs/courses/CALENDAR.md). Session length is 40 minutes (ours, untested); every
179: section plan states the set-up and clean-up minutes inside it. A school that meets once a week keeps every unit and
180: every standard on 32 days by merging the days each unit marks. High school levels are 160 course days of 45 minutes,
181: the 120 hours of the national level descriptor (3.2), and 60 hours on the 80-day short path (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk
182: q-fix-arts-1). The 160 in docs/GLOSSARY.md is the school year, not a course's days, so nothing there changes.
183: 
184: National time figures (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-arts-1): each K to 8 course page names its national arts
185: association's recommended weekly time, read at source on the association's own site (a downloaded file is allowed,
186: Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-downloads-1), and our gap (80 minutes a week, 40 on the short path), as health and
187: physical education do, so a school can add time. A figure not read at source stays off the page. Read 2026-11-21,
188: each from the association's opportunity-to-learn standards:
189: 
190: | Discipline | Recommended weekly time, K to 8 | Our gap (80 minutes a week; 40 on the short path) | Source |
191: |---|---|---|---|
