3: Course `dance-grades-3-to-5` (catalog id `dance-3-5`), a band course for grade 3, grade 4 and grade 5, run as three years one after another (year 3, year 4, year 5). Draft frame, written 2026-11-25; the scope and sequence sets the days later, one year at a time, and each year's part of this file is that year's frame (data/courses.csv, "one frame per grade year").
4: 
5: ## The course in brief
6: 
7: - **Units.** 12: four in each year. Every year has the same four-unit shape, so a thread comes back one step
8:   harder a year later in the same place: a body, space and rhythm unit (technique and the dance elements), a
9:   sources and settings unit (ideas and connecting), a form and response unit (the shape of a dance and responding
10:   to dances), and a performance unit that revises the year's dance and stages it for families (revising and the
11:   stage). The pairing differs from Dance, grades K to 2 on purpose: in grades 3 to 5 the connecting rows ask for
12:   research that feeds a dance (DA:Cn10.1.3b, DA:Cn10.1.4b, DA:Cn10.1.5b), so they sit with the ideas rows; the
13:   revising rows ask for floor maps, relationship sketches and change logs, so they sit with the stage rows, where
14:   the map and the stage plan are one job. Each unit joins two progression threads, and every unit dances, watches
15:   another artist's dance and shows (bar criteria 4 and 5). Units 1, 5 and 9 claim only performing ids; they touch
16:   responding through the dances they watch and creating through the improvisations the class makes, so every unit
17:   touches at least two artistic processes.
18: - **Days.** 192: 64 a year for three years. Each year: 60 days in its four units, 2 course-level check days (one
19:   mid-year, one year-end) and 2 respond days, one after each check. One session of 40 minutes, two sessions a week,
20:   32 weeks a year (STANDARD.md 3.4: a K to 8 arts year is 64 course days; it never needs a daily period, bar
21:   criterion 9). The 40 minutes hold the set-up (tables pushed back, the floor checked and the spots marked, about 3
22:   minutes), a warm-up (about 5), the teaching and dancing, a cool-down and the closing line (about 4) and putting the
23:   room back and collecting cards and props (about 3); a readiness or retrieval task takes ten minutes. Each unit's
24:   days include its end-of-unit assessment (and, in units over 25 days, its mid-unit assessments; no unit here is
25:   over 25 days) and one respond day after it. Unit counts follow the rows' load: years 3 and 4 give 16 days to each
26:   of the first three units (six, five and six ids, each with two check days) and 12 to the performance unit (four
27:   ids, and its dance already made in the unit before). Year 5 gives 17 to unit 9 (a run of phrases rehearsed as an
28:   ensemble against goals set from feedback, which needs a second rehearsal cycle) and 17 to unit 10 (material from
29:   several sources plus a group dance on another subject's topic with a comparison), and 14 to unit 11, whose tools
30:   and forms return from grades 3 and 4, so its study builds faster. These are course days: the school calendar
31:   (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md) spreads them two a week, so course day n of a year falls in that year's week n / 2,
32:   rounded up. National time figure (STANDARD.md 3.4): the dance educators' association's opportunity-to-learn
33:   standards (2017 revision, posted on nationalartsstandards.org) ask for at least 90 minutes of dance a week in
34:   grades 3 to 5. This course gives 80 minutes a week, 10 short of it, and 40 on the once-a-week path, 50 short. A
35:   school can add time. The course is written so a classroom teacher can teach it; a school with a dance specialist
36:   uses that teacher.
37: - **The once-a-week path.** A school that meets once a week keeps every unit and every id in 32 sessions a year by
38:   merging the days each unit marks (STANDARD.md 3.4). Each unit keeps, in this order and no fewer: the
39:   demonstration with a step card, opened by the watch of a dance that carries the unit's response and its connecting
40:   sentence; one rehearsal day with the critique; the showing (the check); one respond day. Fluency days fold into
41:   the warm-ups, and the two rows' understanding days merge into one. Sessions on that path: years 3 and 4, units of
42:   8, 8, 7 and 5 sessions; year 5, units of 9, 8, 6 and 5; each year's two checks keep 1 check session and 1 respond
43:   session. The check sessions fall in the same weeks as on the full path (weeks 17 and 26 in years 3 and 4; weeks 18
44:   and 26 in year 5); the respond sessions fall one week later (weeks 18 and 27 in years 3 and 4; weeks 19 and 27 in
45:   year 5). A respond session is not a check day; in grade 4, week 27 also holds social studies' year-end check, and
46:   the respond session asks no test of any child. A merged day never drops a critique, a rehearsal before a showing
47:   or a respond day; the scope and sequence marks which days merge.
