49:   line in data/standards/ncas-performance-standards-words.txt, never quoted (STANDARD.md 2). The band-year rule
50:   binds (STANDARD.md 3.1): year 3 claims only the 21 grade 3 ids (`.3a`, `.3b`, `.3c`), year 4 only the 21 grade 4
51:   ids, year 5 only the 21 grade 5 ids: 63 in all, the count in STANDARD.md 3.2, every one taught and assessed in its
52:   own year. A year revisits the year before's ids as practice that returns, never as a claim. Every year touches
53:   all four artistic processes (creating, performing, responding, connecting; bar criterion 1). In the Units table
54:   the seventh column is headed "Processes, anchors and works": the artistic processes and anchor standards the unit
55:   works on, and the dances it studies. Each id's action was checked against its words-file line and the row; where a
56:   row's description is narrower than its ids (seven rows), the unit teaches and checks the full id and the row change
57:   is listed for the lead. Check set: each unit plan checks itself against the words-file lines of its ids, the nine
58:   criteria of the arts part of docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md and the lens's error list (docs/agents/lenses/arts.md);
59:   the "Check set adds" column names what that is expected to add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds.
60:   scripts/standards.py checks every id against the id file; each year's scope and sequence still states its id count
61:   (21) against STANDARD.md 3.2.
62: - **Mixed-grade classes.** A school that teaches the band as one mixed class runs the three years in turn (STANDARD.md
63:   3.1); each year still claims only its grade's ids, so a student's record shows which grade's standards they met.
64:   The scope and sequence says this once, on its first page.
65: - **Dance here, motor skill in physical education.** Dance in this course is the art: making, performing,
66:   responding to and connecting dance. Dance steps, chains and routines as motor skill are physical education's
67:   (STANDARD.md 6; physical education grades 3 to 5 units 8, 15 and 22). This course teaches no motor cue: it uses
68:   the moves a student already has (walking, stepping, turning, reaching, small jumps) as material for shapes,
69:   patterns, studies and dances, and teaches choreography, performing for an audience and response. Units 1, 5 and 9
70:   name the physical education rows they lean on (Cross-subject, below).
71: - **Base text.** None. Dance is Curio Class's own (docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md; the open-material scout,
72:   docs/reviews/arts-open-material-2026-11-16.md, found few open dance recordings). Every dance studied here is
73:   described in our own words and linked to the maker's, the company's, a museum's, an archive's or a cultural
74:   body's own page, never copied or embedded (APPROACH "Rights"). Each dance named below is a pick the unit plan keeps
75:   unless its rights line, checked on the item's own page, fails; a swapped dance must keep the unit's ids met and
76:   the band's range. The band's dances come from Africa (Senegal, South Africa, Guinea), the Americas (the United
77:   States, Puerto Rico, Brazil, Canada, Martinique through Katherine Dunham's research, and Native nations through
78:   Dancing Earth and Maria Tallchief of the Osage Nation), Asia (India, Japan, Indonesia, Taiwan), Europe (Ireland,
79:   Germany, the United Kingdom) and the Pacific (Samoa), from 1922 to today, with living makers or living traditions
80:   in every year (APPROACH "Works and their settings"). None repeats a Dance, grades K to 2 pick or a physical
