303: ## Units
304: 
305: | # | Year | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Processes, anchors and works | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
306: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
307: | 1 | 3 | Space, time and energy. *How do dancers use space, time and energy, and how do partners keep a dance safe and together?* | 16 | da-g3-technique; da-g3-elements | DA:Pr5.1.3a; DA:Pr5.1.3b; DA:Pr5.1.3c; DA:Pr4.1.3a; DA:Pr4.1.3b; DA:Pr4.1.3c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding touched by watching two dances (look-and-say as review, no claim); creating touched by the class's improvisations. Works: a solo by Germaine Acogny the unit plan picks (born 1944 in Benin, works in Senegal, where she founded the A%cole des Sables at Toubab Dialaw in 1998; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), moves that start from a wave of the spine with the body aligned and strong, described and linked to the school's own page; a Pilobolus piece the unit plan picks (United States, founded 1971, a company that dances today), dancers making shapes in all three dimensions and shaping the empty space between them, described and linked to the company's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 2's pathways and adjusting (da-g2-technique) and direction, phrases and strong beats (da-g2-elements), each with its ten-minute reteach. DA:Pr5.1.3a: copy a sequence of shapes, qualities and patterns with the body aligned and strong (the alignment check). DA:Pr5.1.3b: with a partner or a group, change level, direction and path safely (the partner signal: look, nod, go). DA:Pr5.1.3c: recall a sequence in pairs or groups, using a coach's one comment and the student's own self-check to improve it (the recall card). DA:Pr4.1.3a: judge the distance between dancers, move in all three dimensions (up and down, side to side, forward and back), shape filled and empty space, and dance with focus (eyes lead the move). DA:Pr4.1.3b: improvise to fill a set count (8 or 16) while staying with the music's timing and its beat phrasing. DA:Pr4.1.3c: vary energy and dynamics (strong, light, sharp, smooth) to give moves a clear quality for the dance's intent. Words: align, dimension, focus, phrasing, dynamics, energy. Showing: each group's copied sequence and improvisation for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two showing days), id by id: day 1, DA:Pr5.1.3a, a look-for: the copied sequence danced with the body aligned and strong; DA:Pr5.1.3b, a look-for: a level, direction and path change made with a partner, signaled and spaced; DA:Pr5.1.3c, a look-for and a kept card: the group recalls the sequence with no teacher cue, and the recall card shows the coach's comment, the self-check and the change tried; day 2, DA:Pr4.1.3a, a look-for: distance kept, all three dimensions used, filled and empty space shaped, eyes leading; DA:Pr4.1.3b, a look-for: the improvisation fills the set count and ends on its last beat with the music; DA:Pr4.1.3c, a look-for: two clearly different energy qualities. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: forms (unit 3), eye contact and drum parts (grade 4). Tools: the alignment card, partner signal card, recall card and elements card, Curio Class's own; a beat keeper on the teacher device or a hand drum. Free path: floor tape or chalk spots, a pot and spoon for the beat. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 3 because every later unit dances in shared space with the warm-up, the stop cue and partner safety, and needs the space, time and energy words; it reopens the dance space after the summer. Six ids, all performed and marked live, so it takes two showing days. The phrase-recall model starts here | Candidate: a seated version of each look-for for a student who cannot move freely | Hazards and controls: floor cleared and checked before each session; bare feet or soft-soled shoes, never socks on a smooth floor; an arm's length between dancers when traveling; level changes to the floor slow, knees and hands first, no dropping; no lifts and no weight-sharing (Pilobolus's lifts are watched only, and the class's shapes are made on each student's own spot); partner contact only by agreement and never required; the stop cue every session. Connection: physical education grades 3 to 5 unit 8 (g3-dance-steps) teaches dance steps as motor skill in grade 3 weeks 30 to 32; this unit teaches no motor cue. Acogny: the sensitivity agent reads the A%cole des Sables' own pages and the artist's published words first; students make their own aligned shapes and do not copy her technique as a style. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
308: | 2 | 3 | Where dances come from. *Where do dancers find ideas, and what can a dance tell us about people, their relationships and what they care about?* | 16 | da-g3-ideas; da-g3-connect | DA:Cr1.1.3a; DA:Cr1.1.3b; DA:Cn10.1.3a; DA:Cn10.1.3b; DA:Cn11.1.3a | Creating (Cr1); connecting (Cn10, Cn11); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Katherine Dunham, L'Ag'Ya (United States, 1938; Dunham, 1909 to 2006), a stage dance she made after her 1935 and 1936 research in the Caribbean, from a fighting dance of Martinique, described and linked to the Library of Congress's or a museum's page; Paul Taylor, Esplanade (United States, 1975; Taylor died 2018), dancers walking, running and sliding together as friends and a group, built from everyday moves, described and linked to the company's page; a Dancing Earth piece about caring for water or seeds that the unit plan picks from the company's own pages (Dancing Earth Indigenous Contemporary Dance Creations, founded by Rulan Tangen in 2004, dancers from many Native nations, a company that dances today), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 2's idea sources and linked moves (da-g2-ideas) and a dance's people and place (da-g2-connect), each with its ten-minute reteach. DA:Cr1.1.3a: pick a starting point of the student's own (a picture, a poem, a sound, an object, a place) and try moves from it, on the student's starting-point list. DA:Cr1.1.3b: take on a movement challenge the teacher sets (cross the floor so your feet never move at the same speed twice; make three shapes, each on different body parts) and choose and show one solution with a one-line reason. DA:Cn10.1.3a: compare the relationships shown in a dance (Esplanade's friends and group) with the student's own relationships, or an invented child's or a story character's, marked the same. DA:Cn10.1.3b: ask a research question about a dance that takes a view on an issue (the Dancing Earth piece and caring for water or seeds), find the answer on Curio Class's research cards, written from the company's own pages and a public agency's page on the issue, explore the key idea in movement, and share what the moves helped them remember or notice. DA:Cn11.1.3a: link a dance's moves to the culture they come from and say what they reveal (L'Ag'Ya's moves and the Martinique fighting dance Dunham studied). Words: starting point, challenge, solution, relationship, research, culture. Showing: the challenge solutions and the research moves shown to the class. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Cr1.1.3a, a look-for: moves made from the student's own starting point, named on the kept starting-point card; DA:Cr1.1.3b, a look-for: the chosen solution to a new challenge card shown, with its one-line reason on the card; DA:Cn10.1.3b, a look-for and a kept card: the research moves shown, and the research card with the question, the answer found and one line on what the moves helped them notice; DA:Cn10.1.3a, a written item: the relationship in an unseen described dance compared with one of the student's own or the invented child's (marked the same); DA:Cn11.1.3a, a written item: two moves of an unseen described dance linked to the culture on its work card, and what they show. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a dance on a school topic from several sources (grade 4), inventing a challenge (grade 4). Tools: the starting-point list, challenge cards, research cards, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: pictures from old magazines, found objects, poems read aloud. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 1 because making from a starting point uses the space, time and energy words and partner safety; before unit 3 because a phrase that shows a feeling or idea needs an idea of the student's own, and comparing two traditions needs this unit's habit of linking moves to their culture. The movement-challenge model starts here | Candidate: a research card set that keeps the issue a question a grade 3 student can answer from two short cards | Ties to the student's own life are invited, never required: the invented child's line is offered beside it and marked the same; health's steps if a student tells something. Dancing Earth: the sensitivity agent reads the company's own published words and a Native-run source first; dancers are named by their own nations as the company names them; the class studies the piece's view on water or seeds by watching, and no student imitates it or wears regalia; no ceremony is studied. Dunham also studied ceremonies in Haiti; the unit uses L'Ag'Ya only, a stage work, and no ceremony; the fighting dance's moves are watched, and students' own moves never strike or touch. The issue stays a school-safe question (caring for water or seeds), and no student is asked to take a side in a public dispute. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; challenge cards ask for nothing higher than a small jump on the floor. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
309: | 3 | 3 | Patterns, forms and styles. *How do repeated and reversed moves give a dance its form, and how do two traditions use pattern differently?* | 16 | da-g3-form; da-g3-respond | DA:Cr2.1.3a; DA:Cr2.1.3b; DA:Re7.1.3a; DA:Re7.1.3b; DA:Re8.1.3a; DA:Re9.1.3a | Creating (Cr2); responding (Re7, Re8, Re9); connecting practiced through each tradition's setting (review). Works: Trisha Brown, Accumulation (United States, 1971; Brown died 2017), one move added at a time and the whole repeated, described and linked to the company's page; a kathak solo by a named living dancer the unit plan picks (northern India, a classical dance grown from storytellers and later the royal courts), a non-devotional piece of footwork patterns and spins, described and linked to the dancer's or a cultural body's page; an Irish step dance piece the unit plan picks (Ireland, danced today; the stage show Riverdance first danced in Dublin in 1994 carried it worldwide), described and linked: two traditions of footwork patterns, one with telling arms and spins, one with still arms | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 2's central idea in the middle (da-g2-form) and clues to meaning across styles (da-g2-respond), each with its ten-minute reteach. DA:Cr2.1.3a: try choreographic tools (repetition, reversal, accumulation) to make simple patterns and AB and ABA forms. DA:Cr2.1.3b: make a phrase that shows a feeling or idea, then discuss how the choices helped show it. DA:Re7.1.3a: spot how a pattern of moves builds one phrase (Accumulation). DA:Re7.1.3b: show and explain how two traditions differ (kathak and Irish step dance: the arms, the upper body, the rhythm of the feet), the showing done in the class's own phrase danced two ways, from step cards written from a named practitioner's published teaching. DA:Re8.1.3a: pick movement clues and explain how they tie to the dance's main idea. DA:Re9.1.3a: pick moves from the two traditions and describe what is alike and different. Class talk practices ELA's SL.3.1 (not owned). Words: pattern, tool, AB, ABA, phrase, style. Showing: each group's ABA dance for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Cr2.1.3a, a look-for: an AB or ABA form danced, with a tool named on the kept form card; DA:Cr2.1.3b, a look-for and a kept line: the phrase shows its feeling or idea, and the line says how one choice helped; DA:Re7.1.3a, a written item: the pattern that builds the teacher's unseen phrase, in move order; DA:Re8.1.3a, a written item: a clue in the unseen phrase and how it ties to its main idea; DA:Re9.1.3a, a written item: one way the two traditions' moves are alike and one way they differ, from two new work cards; DA:Re7.1.3b, a show item: the class phrase shown both ways at the teacher's spot, with one difference told. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: altering a tool for a study around a main idea (grade 4), styles inside one genre (grade 4). Tools: the tools card, the form card, a two-traditions chart, the response card, Curio Class's own. Free path: claps for the rhythm. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 2 because a phrase that shows a feeling or idea starts from a starting point, and comparing two traditions uses unit 2's link between moves and culture; before unit 4 because the dance revised and staged there is the ABA dance made here. The choreographic-tools model starts here | Candidate: a written-item version read aloud a second time for a student who reads below grade 3 | Kathak: its temple and court history in one plain sentence from the named dancer's or a cultural body's page, read first by the sensitivity agent; a non-devotional piece only; no bells or costume, and no hand gesture of devotional meaning is copied (leave-out line). Irish step dance: no hard shoes; stamping soft in bare feet. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; spins slow, three at most in a row, and stop on the cue (dizziness). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
310: | 4 | 3 | Our dance on a stage. *How do dancers change a dance after feedback, and how do stage areas and a design choice help an audience see it?* | 12 | da-g3-revise; da-g3-stage | DA:Cr3.1.3a; DA:Cr3.1.3b; DA:Pr6.1.3a; DA:Pr6.1.3b | Creating (Cr3); performing (Pr6); responding practiced on the two works (review). Works: Oskar Schlemmer, Triadic Ballet (Germany, first danced in 1922; Schlemmer died 1943), costumes built as geometric shapes that change how a body can move, described and linked to a museum's page; kabuki dance (Japan, a theater form begun in the early 1600s, on UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage since 2008), Fuji Musume (The Wisteria Maiden, first staged about 1826), with painted scenery, live shamisen music and a costume change on stage, described and linked to a Japanese cultural body's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 2's chosen change and level symbols (da-g2-revise) and zones and a staging extra (da-g2-stage), each with its ten-minute reteach. DA:Cr3.1.3a: use a partner's feedback (describe, ask, suggest) to change moves in the unit 3 dance, then describe what changed. DA:Cr3.1.3b: chart the dance's travel directions and paths on a floor map with symbols. DA:Pr6.1.3a: name the areas of a stage with production terms (stage left, stage right, center, upstage, downstage) and use them. DA:Pr6.1.3b: try a simple costume piece, music, scenery or light in the dance for its set space, and keep one. Words: feedback, change, map, stage left, stage right, scenery. Showing: the year's dance for families on a marked stage, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (one showing day and kept cards), id by id: DA:Cr3.1.3a, a kept card and a look-for: the change card (the partner's suggestion, the change, one line describing it), and the change seen in the showing; DA:Cr3.1.3b, a kept map: the floor map's directions and paths match the dance; DA:Pr6.1.3a, a kept sketch and a look-for: the stage sketch labeled with five areas, and each dancer starting and finishing in the named area; DA:Pr6.1.3b, a look-for: the design choice kept is used in the dance and named on the program card. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: planning a plain room as a stage (grade 4), relationship sketches (grade 4). Tools: the feedback card, floor map sheet, stage map card and program card, Curio Class's own. Free path: paper costume pieces, a painted paper backdrop, a flashlight, a song sung by the class. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in grade 3 because the dance changed and staged is unit 3's ABA dance; the year-end check sits before it (week 26), outside the core checks of weeks 28 to 32, and this unit's family showing in weeks 27 to 32 is its own assessment. The feedback-loop and stage-terms models start here | Candidate: the program card written by the students, one line each, read aloud at the showing | Kabuki: every role in kabuki is played by men, said plainly if asked; no kimono, wig or face paint, no imitation of Japanese people; the sensitivity agent reads a Japanese cultural body's page first. Schlemmer's costumes: students' costume pieces are paper or card worn over clothes, never over the face or eyes, and nothing that hides the floor from the dancer. Light: flashlights only, never pointed at eyes, no flashing. Family yes for showing outside class; no photographs without it. Hazards and controls: as unit 1. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
311: | 5 | 4 | Rhythm, balance and cues. *How do dancers balance, move together on a cue and answer a drum, and how do they keep their bodies strong and safe?* | 16 | da-g4-technique; da-g4-elements | DA:Pr5.1.4a; DA:Pr5.1.4b; DA:Pr5.1.4c; DA:Pr4.1.4a; DA:Pr4.1.4b; DA:Pr4.1.4c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding touched on the two works (review); creating touched by the drum-and-dance pairs. Works: bomba (Puerto Rico, an Afro-Puerto Rican tradition danced today), where a dancer's sharp moves call the lead drum and the drummer answers each one, a piece by a named living group the unit plan picks, described and linked to a Puerto Rican cultural body's or a museum's page; legong (Bali, Indonesia, a dance made for watching, taught to young dancers today; on UNESCO's list since 2015 among Bali's three genres of traditional dance), with held balances, exact fingers and quick moves of the eyes, described and linked to a cultural body's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 1's aligned sequence and recall (da-g3-technique) and space, time and energy (da-g3-elements). DA:Pr5.1.4a: use alignment and balance when copying and recalling movement patterns. DA:Pr5.1.4b: do a short strengthening routine (wall push, knee plank, calf rises) and explain how technique, avoiding injury and food choices connect, through an invented dancer's week on a card. DA:Pr5.1.4c: match phrases with the other dancers on a music cue or a held-card cue, and use feedback to set the group's next goal. DA:Pr4.1.4a: make fixed and changing shapes, land small jumps softly, and connect with a partner through eye contact. DA:Pr4.1.4b: play a simple drum part for others to dance to, move to steady and uneven rhythms, and react when the tempo changes. DA:Pr4.1.4c: analyze the energy changes in a phrase, describe them in vivid words, then refine the phrase. Words: balance, strengthen, cue, rhythm, tempo, energy. Showing: drum-and-dance pairs for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Pr5.1.4a, a look-for: a balance held in the copied and recalled pattern; DA:Pr5.1.4c, a look-for and a kept card: the group starts and changes together on the cue, and the goal card names a goal set from feedback; DA:Pr4.1.4a, a look-for: a fixed and a changing shape, soft landings, eye contact with a partner; DA:Pr4.1.4b, a look-for: each student plays the group's drum part once and dances once through a tempo change; DA:Pr5.1.4b, a written item and a show item: from a new invented dancer's card, how her warm-up, technique and food choices connect to avoiding injury, and two moves of the strengthening routine shown with alignment at the teacher's spot; DA:Pr4.1.4c, a written item: the energy changes in the teacher's unseen phrase in two vivid words, and one refinement for the class phrase. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: tight against flowing (grade 5). Tools: the rhythm card (steady and uneven patterns in claps), the cue card, the energy word bank and the invented dancer card, Curio Class's own; a beat keeper or a hand drum. Free path: a pot and wooden spoon or a box drum; a sheet of card raised as the cue. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 4 because it reopens the dance space, and unit 6's challenges are solved by changing elements, which needs this unit's balance, rhythm and energy; the energy-words model starts here and unit 7's responses use it. Six ids, all performed except two to explain, so a showing day and a written day | Candidate: the invented dancer card read by the health course's lens for its food line | Food: no task asks about a student's own diet, weight or body; food choices and eating are studied through the invented dancer only (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"). Bomba: its roots among enslaved Africans on Puerto Rico's sugar plantations are said in one plain sentence from a Puerto Rican cultural body's or a museum's page, read first by the sensitivity agent; no skirt work or costume; students make their own call-and-answer moves. Legong: Bali also has sacred dances that are never danced for show; legong is a dance made for watching, studied by watching, no costume or headdress, and eye moves are tried only as "focus", not copied as a style (leave-out line). Hazards and controls: as unit 1; small jumps on the student's own spot, landing softly through the feet and knees; no plank held more than 20 seconds; the drum played at a moderate volume. Connection: physical education grades 3 to 5 unit 15 (g4-dance-sequence) teaches dance sequences as motor skill in grade 4 weeks 27 to 30; this unit teaches no motor cue. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
312: | 6 | 4 | Dances from facts and places. *How can facts from a school subject become a dance, and how does a dance style carry the place and time it comes from?* | 16 | da-g4-ideas; da-g4-connect | DA:Cr1.1.4a; DA:Cr1.1.4b; DA:Cn10.1.4a; DA:Cn10.1.4b; DA:Cn11.1.4a | Creating (Cr1); connecting (Cn10, Cn11); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: the gumboot dance (South Africa, made by Black mine workers in the gold mines around Johannesburg in the twentieth century, danced today), boots slapped and stamped in patterns, a piece by a named living group the unit plan picks, described and linked to a South African cultural body's or a museum's page; Liz Lerman, Ferocious Beauty: Genome (United States, 2006; Lerman born 1947, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a dance she made by working with scientists, described and linked to the artist's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 2's own starting points and challenge (da-g3-ideas) and research and culture ties (da-g3-connect). DA:Cr1.1.4a: name choreography ideas from music, pictures and experiences on an idea card. DA:Cr1.1.4b: invent a movement challenge for a partner, solved by changing dance elements. DA:Cn10.1.4a: link a watched dance's main idea to the student's own experience, or an invented child's, marked the same, and say how they match or differ. DA:Cn10.1.4b: research a school topic in two or three sources (from science grade 4's units taught by then: speed and collisions, energy on the move, or waves), choreograph its key facts as a group, and reflect on what dancing them showed. DA:Cn11.1.4a: describe the gumboot dance's moves and explain how they reflect the mines, the workers and their time. Words: choreography, inspiration, challenge, source, fact, era. Showing: each group's topic dance with its source list. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Cr1.1.4a, a kept card: the idea card with three ideas, each from a different kind of inspiration; DA:Cr1.1.4b, a look-for and a kept card: the challenge the student invented and the partner's solution shown, the element changed named on the card; DA:Cn10.1.4b, a look-for and kept notes: two or three key facts danced, each source named on the research notes, one reflection line; DA:Cn10.1.4a, a written item: an unseen described dance's main idea linked to an experience (the student's own or the invented child's), with how they are alike or different; DA:Cn11.1.4a, a written item: two moves of an unseen style on a new work card and how they reflect its place, era or community. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: several sources into one body of material and learning compared across subjects (grade 5). Tools: the idea card, challenge card, research notes sheet, source cards Curio Class writes from the science course's own sourced readings, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: pictures and music the class already has. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 5 because an invented challenge is solved by changing the elements and rhythms just taught; before unit 7 because the study around one main idea starts from an idea card. The research-notes model starts here, and the topics come from science units taught by these weeks | Candidate: the source cards checked against the science course's readings, fact by fact | Gumboot dance: made by migrant mine workers who lived far from home under hard and unfair rules, said in one plain sentence from a South African cultural body's or a museum's page, read first by the sensitivity agent; no boots, no imitation of a people; students slap their own thighs gently and stamp softly in bare feet. Lerman: the work is studied only for how she worked with scientists; genes are not taught. Ties to the student's own life invited, never required, with the invented option; health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: as unit 1. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
313: | 7 | 4 | Styles inside one genre. *How do patterns shape a style or a theme across dances, and what makes a dance artistic?* | 16 | da-g4-form; da-g4-respond | DA:Cr2.1.4a; DA:Cr2.1.4b; DA:Re7.1.4a; DA:Re7.1.4b; DA:Re8.1.4a; DA:Re9.1.4a | Creating (Cr2); responding (Re7, Re8, Re9); connecting practiced through each style's setting (review). Works: locking (Los Angeles, about 1970, begun by Don Campbell, who died 2020) and popping (Fresno, California, 1970s, from the Electric Boogaloos), two styles inside hip-hop dance, each a piece by a named dancer the unit plan picks, described and linked to the dancer's or a museum's page; Merce Cunningham, Beach Birds (United States, 1991; Cunningham died 2009), a dance of birds at the shore whose order he set partly by chance, described and linked to the Cunningham trust's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 3's tools and forms (da-g3-form) and two traditions compared (da-g3-respond). DA:Cr2.1.4a: alter choreographic tools (repeat, reverse, add a level, change the order by a chance draw, as Cunningham did) to widen pattern and form choices, and discuss the choices. DA:Cr2.1.4b: build a dance study around one main idea and discuss how well the choices worked. DA:Re7.1.4a: spot the movement patterns across dances that shape a theme or a style. DA:Re7.1.4b: show and tell how locking and popping differ inside one genre, the showing done in the class's own phrase danced two ways from step cards written from named dancers' published teaching. DA:Re8.1.4a: decode a dance's meaning from its moves, its ideas and its setting together, in basic terms. DA:Re9.1.4a: discuss the traits that make a dance artistic, then apply them to dances watched. Class talk practices ELA's SL.4.1 (not owned). Words: theme, style, genre, setting, study, artistic. Showing: each group's dance study for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Cr2.1.4a, a look-for and a kept card: a tool altered on purpose in the study, named on the tools card with what it opened up; DA:Cr2.1.4b, a look-for and a kept line: the study's main idea is clear, and the line says how one choice worked; DA:Re7.1.4a, a written item: the pattern that carries one theme across two unseen phrases the teacher dances; DA:Re8.1.4a, a written item: the meaning of an unseen described dance from its moves, idea and setting on its work card; DA:Re9.1.4a, a written item: two traits of an artistic dance applied to the unseen dance ("It is artistic because ___, shown when ___"); DA:Re7.1.4b, a show item: the class phrase shown as locking and as popping, with one difference told. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: several tools altered together (grade 5). Tools: the tools card, chance cards (numbered cards drawn to set an order), the reason card and the response card, Curio Class's own. Free path: numbered scraps of paper drawn from a cup. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 6 because the study grows from the idea card, and locking and popping are described with unit 5's energy words; before unit 8 because the study is reworked and staged there. The dance-reason-because model starts here | Candidate: a third style inside one genre, from outside the United States, as an extension card | Hip-hop: locking and popping were begun by Black and Latino young people in California; the dancers are named as their own pages name them; no costumes; the moves are tried as the class's own phrase after the dancers' published teaching. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; no floor spins, flips or other inverted moves; popping's muscle hits are small and never held. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
314: | 8 | 4 | Making a room a stage. *How do dancers rework a dance from comments, and how can a gym or a lawn feel like a real stage?* | 12 | da-g4-revise; da-g4-stage | DA:Cr3.1.4a; DA:Cr3.1.4b; DA:Pr6.1.4a; DA:Pr6.1.4b | Creating (Cr3); performing (Pr6); responding practiced on the two works (review). Works: George Balanchine, The Firebird (United States, 1949, New York City Ballet), danced by Maria Tallchief (Osage Nation, 1925 to 2013), with costumes and scenery by Marc Chagall, described and linked to the company's or a museum's page; Urban Bush Women (United States, founded by Jawole Willa Jo Zollar in 1984, a company that dances today), dances made with and for communities in parks, streets and community halls, described and linked to the company's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 4's feedback and floor map (da-g3-revise) and stage areas and design choice (da-g3-stage). DA:Cr3.1.4a: rework the unit 7 study using classmates' comments and the student's own reflection to sharpen its intent, and explain the choices. DA:Cr3.1.4b: sketch how dancers stand in relation to each other (beside, in front, behind, above on a higher level). DA:Pr6.1.4a: plan how to make the gym, a hall or a lawn feel like a formal stage (where the audience sits, the entrances, a marked edge, a backdrop), paced out in steps, no measuring. DA:Pr6.1.4b: try different production elements (costume, music, scenery, a prop, light) and keep the one that best strengthens the dance's purpose for the audience. Words: rework, reflection, intent, relationship, formal stage, production. Showing: the year's dance for families in the planned space, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (one showing day and kept cards), id by id: DA:Cr3.1.4a, a kept card and a look-for: the rework card (two comments, the student's own reflection, the change and why), and the change seen in the showing; DA:Cr3.1.4b, a kept sketch: three dancers' relationships drawn and labeled for one moment of the dance; DA:Pr6.1.4a, a kept plan and a look-for: the stage plan, and the showing set up as planned; DA:Pr6.1.4b, a look-for and a kept line: the production element kept is used, and the program card says how it serves the dance's purpose. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: one dance fitted to two venues (grade 5), a change log (grade 5). Tools: the rework card, relationship sketch sheet, stage plan sheet and program card, Curio Class's own. Free path: a painted paper backdrop, chairs for the audience edge, a song or a drum. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in grade 4 because the dance reworked and staged is unit 7's study; the year-end check sits before it (week 26), outside the core checks of weeks 27 and 30 to 32, and this unit's family showing in weeks 27 to 32 is its own assessment | Candidate: a seated audience plan for a family member who uses a wheelchair, in every stage plan | Tallchief: of the Osage Nation, named as her own published words name her; the sensitivity agent reads the company's or a museum's page and an Osage Nation source first; no costume or imitation of the Firebird or of a people. Outdoors: the lawn checked for holes, sticks and glass; shade and water; the class stays in sight; bare feet only on checked grass. Light: no flashing, never pointed at eyes. Family yes for showing outside class; no photographs without it. Hazards and controls: as unit 1. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
315: | 9 | 5 | Rhythm from the body and the world. *How do dancers make rhythm with their bodies and the sounds around them, and how does an ensemble keep together and safe?* | 17 | da-g5-technique; da-g5-elements | DA:Pr5.1.5a; DA:Pr5.1.5b; DA:Pr5.1.5c; DA:Pr4.1.5a; DA:Pr4.1.5b; DA:Pr4.1.5c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding touched on the works (review); creating touched by the ensemble's body-percussion phrase. Works: sasa (Samoa, a group dance danced today, often seated, with fast, tight slaps, claps and arm moves in unison to a drum), a piece by a named group the unit plan picks, described and linked to a Samoan cultural body's or a museum's page; Doris Humphrey, Water Study (United States, 1928; Humphrey died 1958), flowing waves of movement begun in the torso, danced to no music but the dancers' breath, described and linked to an archive's page; Stomp (United Kingdom, first staged in Brighton in 1991 by Luke Cresswell and Steve McNicholas, performed today), rhythm made with brooms, tubs and everyday things, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 5's balance, cues and strengthening (da-g4-technique) and drum parts, eye contact and energy words (da-g4-elements). DA:Pr5.1.5a: recall and perform a run of phrases with alignment and clarity. DA:Pr5.1.5b: practice safe body habits (warm-up, soft landings, rest, water) and discuss how they and nutrition help prevent injury, through an invented dancer. DA:Pr5.1.5c: rehearse with the ensemble to synchronize timing and refine spacing, and set goals from feedback (the goal card). DA:Pr4.1.5a: combine shapes with ground paths and air paths (small jumps and leaps), connect through eye focus and project outward to an audience. DA:Pr4.1.5b: dance to rhythms from inside the body (claps, slaps, stamps, breath) and from outside it (found sounds), and respond when the timing changes. DA:Pr4.1.5c: contrast tight and flowing movement started from the torso or the limbs, and analyze the energy. Words: ensemble, synchronize, project, body percussion, tight, flowing. Showing: the ensemble's body-percussion dance for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Pr5.1.5a, a look-for: a run of three phrases recalled with alignment and clarity; DA:Pr5.1.5c, a look-for and a kept card: unison timing and even spacing, and the goal card's goal set from feedback; DA:Pr4.1.5a, a look-for: ground and air paths with eye focus and projection; DA:Pr4.1.5b, a look-for: the group dances to its own body percussion and a found-sound rhythm and keeps on when the teacher changes the timing; DA:Pr5.1.5b, a written item: from a new invented dancer's card, two safe habits and how they and food help prevent injury; DA:Pr4.1.5c, a show item and a written item: one move shown tight and then flowing at the teacher's spot, saying where it starts, and one line analyzing the energy of the teacher's unseen phrase. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: partner and group sections in measures of three and four (grade 6). Tools: the run card, goal card, body-percussion card and invented dancer card, Curio Class's own; a beat keeper or a hand drum. Free path: plastic tubs, paper and the body for found sound. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 5 because it reopens the dance space, and the ensemble habits and goal card carry every later rehearsal of the year; 17 days because a run of phrases rehearsed to goals needs a second rehearsal cycle after the critique. The rehearsal-goals model starts here | Candidate: the found-sound kit checked by the safety agent item by item | Food: no task asks about a student's own diet, weight or body; nutrition is studied through the invented dancer only (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"). Sasa: the sensitivity agent reads a Samoan cultural body's or a museum's page first; no costume; slaps gentle, on the student's own thighs and the floor. Found sound: plastic tubs, paper and soft sticks only; no metal lids, bins or glass; a moderate volume and a rest after loud passages. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; air paths are small jumps and low leaps on a clear path, landing softly; no lifts. Connection: physical education grades 3 to 5 unit 22 (g5-gym-dance-sequence) teaches a routine of dance parts as motor skill in grade 5 weeks 22 to 25; this unit teaches no motor cue. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
316: | 10 | 5 | Many sources, opposing themes. *How do dancers build material from many sources, and how can dances of the same years carry opposite feelings?* | 17 | da-g5-ideas; da-g5-connect | DA:Cr1.1.5a; DA:Cr1.1.5b; DA:Cn10.1.5a; DA:Cn10.1.5b; DA:Cn11.1.5a | Creating (Cr1); connecting (Cn10, Cn11); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Lin Hwai-min, Cursive (Taiwan, 2001, Cloud Gate Dance Theatre of Taiwan, founded 1973; Lin born 1947, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a dance built from brush calligraphy and slow, flowing training, described and linked to the company's page; Pearl Primus, Hard Time Blues (United States, 1943; Primus, born in Trinidad, 1919 to 1994), a solo about the hard lives of Black sharecroppers in the South, with huge leaps, described and linked to an archive's or a museum's page; the Lindy hop at Harlem's Savoy Ballroom in the 1930s (United States; Frankie Manning, 1914 to 2009, among its dancers), a joyful partner social dance, described and linked: two African American dances of the same years with opposing themes | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 6's idea card and invented challenge (da-g4-ideas) and research notes and a style's era (da-g4-connect). DA:Cr1.1.5a: build dance material from several inspirations (a story, nature, a news card Curio Class writes from a public agency's news release), each with its source line. DA:Cr1.1.5b: invent and solve several movement challenges in a small group to build material. DA:Cn10.1.5a: contrast two dances with opposing themes (Hard Time Blues and the Lindy hop), discussing the feelings they raise, with feeling words from a card, and each dance's viewpoint. DA:Cn10.1.5b: research a topic from another subject (science grade 5's particles of matter in solids, liquids and gases), dance it as a group, and compare how dancing it and the science lessons taught it. DA:Cn11.1.5a: describe how a style's movement qualities express its culture's or period's ideas (Cursive's slow flow and calligraphy; the Lindy hop's swing and its time). Words: source, material, theme, viewpoint, quality, period. Showing: each group's matter dance and its source material, for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Cr1.1.5a, a look-for and a kept card: material from three different sources danced, each source named on the card; DA:Cr1.1.5b, a kept log and a look-for: the group's challenge log (three challenges invented and solved) and one solution shown; DA:Cn10.1.5b, a look-for and kept notes: the group's matter dance shows the three states as the research notes record them, with one line comparing learning in dance and in science; DA:Cn10.1.5a, a written item: two unseen described dances with opposing themes, a feeling each raises and each one's viewpoint; DA:Cn11.1.5a, a written item: an unseen style's movement qualities and the culture's or period's ideas on its work card. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: joining ideas by likeness or contrast, a social issue danced (grade 6). Tools: the source card, challenge log, research notes sheet, feeling card and work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: a story the class has read, a nature walk on school ground. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 9 because material is built and rehearsed with the ensemble habits; before unit 11 because the study from chosen moves is chosen from this unit's material; 17 days because a group dance on another subject's topic and its comparison are a second making cycle beside the sources | Candidate: the news card set kept to items with no harm or conflict in them | Hard Time Blues: the hardship and unfair treatment Black sharecroppers faced in the South are said in one plain sentence from a museum's or archive's page, read first by the sensitivity agent; no student plays a sharecropper or acts out hardship; the class watches and describes; feeling words from a card, and no student is asked about their own hard times. Lindy hop: its aerials and throws are watched only; the class's partner moves are danced side by side, holding hands only if both agree, no lifts or throws. Cursive: Taiwan is named as the company names itself; calligraphy is tried with water on paper or in the air, no ink. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; leaps small, landing softly. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
317: | 11 | 5 | How dance speaks without words. *How do dancers shape a study so it speaks without words, and what makes a dance meaningful?* | 14 | da-g5-form; da-g5-respond | DA:Cr2.1.5a; DA:Cr2.1.5b; DA:Re7.1.5a; DA:Re7.1.5b; DA:Re8.1.5a; DA:Re9.1.5a | Creating (Cr2); responding (Re7, Re8, Re9); connecting practiced through the works' settings (review). Works: Crystal Pite, Emergence (Canada, 2009, for the National Ballet of Canada; Pite born 1970, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), dancers moving as a swarm of insects, patterns that carry the meaning, described and linked to the company's page; a staged piece by Les Ballets Africains the unit plan picks (Guinea, founded in Paris in 1952 by FodAcba KeA_ta, Guinea's national company from 1958, dancing today), a piece made for audiences, not a ceremony, described and linked to the company's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 7's study and altered tool (da-g4-form) and styles in a genre, meaning from setting and artistic traits (da-g4-respond). DA:Cr2.1.5a: alter several choreographic tools together to open options and sharpen the central idea, and explain the choices. DA:Cr2.1.5b: build a study from moves chosen from unit 10's material, and discuss how dance says things without words. DA:Re7.1.5a: find a dance's meaning or purpose from its movement patterns (Emergence's swarm). DA:Re7.1.5b: describe a dance style the student chooses, their own culture's if they choose to share it, or one from the class's style cards (marked the same), in basic terms, and compare it with another style. DA:Re8.1.5a: interpret a dance from its movements, explaining how they convey its main idea. DA:Re9.1.5a: define the traits of an artistic, meaningful dance and tie each to a dance element. The written response practices ELA's W.5.1, an opinion with reasons (not owned). Words: central idea, without words, interpret, meaning, purpose, traits. Showing: each group's study for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Cr2.1.5a, a look-for and a kept card: two tools altered together in the study, named on the tools card with how they sharpen the idea; DA:Cr2.1.5b, a look-for and a kept line: the study is built from chosen moves, and the line says how it speaks without words; DA:Re7.1.5b, a kept card made on the last making day: the style card (the chosen style described in three dance terms and compared with one other); DA:Re7.1.5a, a written item: the purpose of the teacher's unseen phrase from its pattern; DA:Re8.1.5a, a written item: an interpretation of an unseen described dance with two moves that carry its main idea; DA:Re9.1.5a, a written item: an opinion naming two traits of a meaningful dance, each tied to an element, with reasons. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a written critique with the student's own criteria (grade 6). Tools: the tools card, style cards, the opinion frame and the response card, Curio Class's own. Free path: as unit 10. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 10 because the study is chosen from its material; 14 days because tools and forms return from grades 3 and 4, so the study builds faster; before unit 12 because the study is the dance refined and staged there | Candidate: a style card set with at least six styles from five regions, so no student must use their own | No student is asked to name, show or explain their own family's culture: the style cards are offered beside it and marked the same. Les Ballets Africains: some Guinean dances come from ceremonies; the unit uses a piece the company presents for audiences, and the sensitivity agent reads the company's own pages and a Guinean cultural body's or a museum's page first; no costume or imitation of a people (leave-out line). Health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; the swarm study keeps an arm's length, no touching in the crowd. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
318: | 12 | 5 | One dance, two spaces. *How do dancers refine a dance from peer suggestions, and how does a dance change to fit two different spaces?* | 12 | da-g5-revise; da-g5-stage | DA:Cr3.1.5a; DA:Cr3.1.5b; DA:Pr6.1.5a; DA:Pr6.1.5b | Creating (Cr3); performing (Pr6); responding practiced on the two works (review). Works: frevo (Recife, Brazil, begun in the late 1800s; on UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage since 2012), danced in the streets at carnival and on stages, with small umbrellas, described and linked to UNESCO's or a Brazilian cultural body's page; Trisha Brown, Roof Piece (United States, 1971), dancers on rooftops across New York passing moves from roof to roof, described and linked to the company's page (Brown from unit 3, a new work in a new space) | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 8's rework and relationship sketch (da-g4-revise) and stage plan and production elements (da-g4-stage). DA:Cr3.1.5a: try peer suggestions in movement to widen the options in the unit 11 study, and explain the refinements kept. DA:Cr3.1.5b: log what changed in the sequence in words, drawings or a recording (the change log). DA:Pr6.1.5a: adapt the dance to two venues (the classroom and the gym, a hall or a lawn) by changing its spacing and moves. DA:Pr6.1.5b: choose staging features that strengthen the dance's purpose and suit both venues. Words: suggestion, refine, log, venue, adapt, staging. Showing: the dance in two spaces, the second for families, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (two showing days and a kept log), id by id: DA:Cr3.1.5a, a look-for and a kept line: a peer suggestion tried and kept is seen in the showing, and the log's line explains the refinement; DA:Cr3.1.5b, a kept log: at least three changes logged in words or drawings, each dated; DA:Pr6.1.5a, a look-for across both showing days: spacing and moves changed to fit each venue; DA:Pr6.1.5b, a look-for and a kept note: the staging features chosen work in both venues, and the staging note says how they serve the purpose. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: performance manners and the choreographer's notes (grade 6). Tools: the suggestion card, change log, venue plan sheet and staging note, Curio Class's own. Free path: paper umbrellas, chairs for the audience edge, the class's voices for music. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in the band because the dance refined and staged is unit 11's study; the year-end check sits before it (week 26), outside the core checks of weeks 29 to 32; the two showings in weeks 27 to 32 are its own assessment, and the thread then goes to grade 6's production terms | Candidate: the second venue offered as a recorded showing for a school with one room only | Frevo: its carnival roots are said from UNESCO's and a Brazilian cultural body's page, read first by the sensitivity agent; no costume; umbrellas small, paper or soft plastic, with no points or metal ribs. Roof Piece: no rooftop, balcony, stage edge or height of any kind; both venues are at ground level. Recording: a change log may be a recording only with the family's yes, of feet or hands, a face never required. Family yes for showing outside class; no photographs without it. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; frevo's quick steps danced low and on the spot, no squats held. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
