e moves taken from each; DA:Cr1.1.6b, a written item on the written day: the styles note, three styles tried on one idea and one feature of each that changed the steps; DA:Cr2.1.6a, a guide look-for on the showing: at least two tools and the form seen, and the goal stated before dancing; DA:Cr2.1.6b, the kept criteria sheet: three criteria and the judged pair of moves with a reason from the criteria. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: sources compared and styles chosen for an aim (grade 7). Tools: the inspiration list, style menu, tools card and criteria sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: picture and sound cards printed, a die for the chance roll. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 1 because a study needs the spacing, accents and tension just taught as its material; before unit 3 because unit 3 revises this study against group criteria. The style-menu model starts here, where it is first needed | Candidate: a study inspiration from a maker outside the United States beside Childs and Robbins | Personal studies are invited, never required: the invented dancer's or public figure's option is offered beside them and marked the same; a cultural study uses a public tradition from a source, never a classmate's family. Fancy Free's sailors flirt and scuffle; the unit uses the three solos only. Health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: canon and traveling in groups keep the marked lanes; the stop cue; floor checked. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
